1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,640
 In this lecture, we'll learn about an AWS AI service called as Amazon Lex, the AI Chatbot.

2
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:15,360
 Amazon Lex allows you to build and deploy conversational AI interfaces.

3
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:24,720
 So, what it means is that with Amazon Lex, you can build chatbots both for voice and text.

4
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:29,520
 Now, let's try and understand some of the use cases for Amazon Lex.

5
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:36,640
 So, with Amazon Lex, you can build self-service voice-assisted and chatbots.

6
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:42,400
 So, basically, you can build a chatbot which will support your call center.

7
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:48,480
 So, now, let's say, customer has some issue with your product and he wants to raise a complaint.

8
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:54,720
 So, he can do it through a chatbot built using the Amazon Lex.

9
00:00:54,720 --> 00:01:00,800
 The next use case is, you can build an application or transaction bot.

10
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:07,520
 So, what this means is, you can build say an application that allows you to order a pizza,

11
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:09,360
 maybe book a hotel or a flight.

12
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:14,720
 Then third one is, you can build an enterprise productivity bot.

13
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:22,000
 So, you can build a chatbot which connects your enterprise data resources which allows your

14
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:29,680
 employees to query the data sources at get a response and then you have the device control bot

15
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:36,960
 which will allow you to use Amazon Lex to issue control commands to the connected device

16
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:40,560
 that you might have within your home or organization.

17
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:44,240
 So, these are some of the use cases for Amazon Lex.

18
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:49,440
 Now, there are some terminologies that you need to understand.

19
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:54,400
 Before, you can build a chatbot using Amazon Lex.

20
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:55,920
 Now, let's take an example.

21
00:01:55,920 --> 00:02:01,120
 So, here is a chatbot that helps end users book a hotel.

22
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:04,080
 So, there are four or five key terms that you need to know.

23
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:11,520
 So, one is the intense, then you have utterances, slots, confirmation and fulfillment.

24
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:15,520
 Now, let's try and understand what each of them means.

25
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:19,920
 So, the first one is the intense.

26
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:26,640
 End user comes to this chatbot with some intent or some goal and in this case, because it's

27
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:31,760
 hotel booking app, he will come with intent to book a hotel.

28
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:39,760
 So, that's the intent and when the user comes with the intent, there would be some downstream

29
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:43,520
 action that would be taken by the bot, okay.

30
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:44,800
 So, that's intent.

31
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:46,880
 The next one is the utterance.

32
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:55,280
 It's the spoken or type phrase by the end user that will invoke your intent.

33
00:02:55,280 --> 00:03:00,240
 Let's say the user says I would like to book a hotel.

34
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:06,800
 So, this is called as an utterance and what is the intent here to book a hotel.

35
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:15,680
 Now, the third one is slots. So, slots are input data required to fulfill the intent.

36
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:22,560
 My intent was to book a hotel and my utterance was I would like to book a hotel.

37
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:29,840
 Now, if I have to book a hotel, I need at least two details from the end user.

38
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:38,400
 One is which city I want to book the hotel and the second one is the date or dates if it's multiple

39
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:44,640
 days. So, once the user has uttered something like I would like to book a hotel with the intent

40
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:53,360
 to book a hotel, next he is presented with some questions called as slots on and in this case,

41
00:03:53,360 --> 00:04:03,200
 which city and which date. So, once the user provides these details, the fourth one is called as

42
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:08,000
 confirmation and this is an example of confirmation. You are summarizing the inputs that he has

43
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,520
 provided you and in this case, you can see I will show you want to book a hotel in New York

44
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:18,480
 and he says yes. And then finally, we have our fifth term, which is fulfillment,

45
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:24,560
 which means that your order has been confirmed. And in this case, you can see here he says,

46
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:31,520
 thank you, the reservation went through successfully. So, what you can also do with Amazon Lexus,

47
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:38,720
 you can integrate it to a data source, maybe DynamoDB, RDS or any other data source via

48
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:43,840
 Lambda function. So, it integrates with the Lambda function. So, when the user provides these inputs,

49
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:49,440
 you can make a hotel booking and then get a confirmation number. Along with the thank you,

50
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:56,640
 you can provide the confirmation details here to the user, maybe booking IID or something on those

51
00:04:56,640 --> 00:05:01,760
 lines. Now, let us go to the console and let me just show you a quick demo.

